course: dictionary
lesson: 3923
title: Frostbitten: a word from the cold
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Frostbitten is a long word, but you can split it into two parts you may know: frost and bitten. Frost is the white ice that forms on cold nights. Bitten here shows that the cold has hurt something, so the word tells you about harm caused by freezing weather.
explain: Read the clues around the word. «Rubbing damages frostbitten skin; she should warm it slowly instead.» The clues are rubbing damages and warm it slowly. Rubbing makes things worse, so the skin is hurt and needs gentle care. Try your meaning in the sentence: "Rubbing damages skin hurt by extreme cold" still makes sense, so you have it right.
explain: Frostbitten is often used with body parts, like «frostbitten fingers» or «frostbitten toes». In winter safety lessons you learn the warning signs: numb, white or hard skin can mean frostbite. If you notice these, warm the skin gently and get help from an adult.
fact: Frostbitten means injured by extreme cold, so that skin and the flesh under it freeze.
fact: Frostbitten often describes fingers, toes, ears and nose.
fact: Frostbitten can also describe plants that have been damaged by frost.
fact: Wear gloves, hats and warm socks in cold weather to help protect yourself.
fact: Numb, white or hard skin can be a sign of frostbite: warm it gently and get help.
exercise: «Rubbing damages frostbitten skin; she should warm it slowly instead.» What does frostbitten describe here? | *Skin hurt by extreme cold | A loud noise in the street | A sweet, sticky fruit | A warm summer day
exercise: Frostbitten plants have been damaged by frost. What has happened to the plants? | They were picked for dinner | They were painted bright green | *They were damaged by frost | They grew very quickly in the sun
exercise: The explorers came back with «frostbitten fingers». What does this tell you? | They won a race | *Their fingers were hurt by extreme cold | Their fingers were covered in paint | Their fingers were very long
test: Numb, white or hard skin can mean frostbite. What should you do first? | Ignore it, because it will go away | Put it straight into boiling water | *Warm it gently and get help | Rub the skin hard with snow
test: In frostbitten, what does frost mean? | *Ice that forms in cold weather | A type of bird | A kind of sweet | A hot, dry wind
exam: Which sentence gives the best meaning of frostbitten? | Bitten by a dog on a winter walk | Covered in sweet frosting and icing | *Injured by extreme cold, so that skin and the flesh under it freeze | Shiny and wet from the rain
